              compression: "10.7:1",
              yearRange: "2004-2010",
              timingType: "Chain-driven DOHC with variable cam timing (The M272.964 uses a modern duplex chain-driven valve train with continuously variable intake and exhaust cam phasing. However, like other early M272 variants, it is defined by the notorious balance shaft and intermediate gear wear issue. The chain system itself is robust when oil quality is kept high, but neglected oil changes accelerate wear in the tensioners, guides, and cam phasers. On this port-injected version, thermal stress is lower than on the later CGI engine, but the balance shaft remains the critical ownership checkpoint. Any serious purchase inspection should verify engine serial range, timing correlation values, and whether the revised hardened balance shaft components have already been installed.)",
              injectionType: "Multi-Point Manifold Injection / Sequential Port Injection (This version uses conventional electronically controlled port injection rather than direct injection. That makes it materially simpler and more tolerant than the later CGI variant. Fuel washes over the intake valves, so carbon buildup is far less severe than on direct-injection engines. Diagnosis and repair costs are generally lower, with the usual weak points being injector imbalance, vacuum leaks, MAF contamination, and oxygen sensor aging rather than high-pressure fuel system failures.)",
              engineLifespan: "250 000 - 350 000 km (The M272.964 can be a durable engine when the balance shaft issue is ruled out or corrected and the cooling and oil systems are maintained properly. The aluminum V6 architecture itself is fundamentally sound, but neglected examples accumulate multiple medium-cost failures such as oil cooler leaks, intake manifold tumble flap wear, engine mount collapse, and timing noise. Well-kept examples remain smooth and refined at high mileage. Cheap cars without proper history are usually a bigger risk than high-mileage cars with documented maintenance.)",
              turboType: "Naturally Aspirated",
              ecuType: "Bosch ME Engine Management (North American and South Korean market calibration with local emissions software and SAE power correction. The ECU controls fueling, ignition timing, variable cam timing, throttle behavior, and emissions monitoring. Common fault areas include camshaft correlation faults, mixture adaptation drift, oxygen sensor aging, secondary air injection issues, and intake manifold tumble flap problems. Tuning support exists, but the naturally aspirated hardware limits major gains.)",
              euroStandard: "North American / South Korean market-specific emissions calibration",
              maxPowerStockInternals: "285 - 295 hp (A conservative ECU tune, intake optimization, and exhaust improvements can produce small gains, but the naturally aspirated M272.964 is not a major tuning platform. The smarter investment is mechanical restoration and preventive maintenance rather than chasing marginal power increases.)",
