              timingType: "Single overhead camshaft, 3-valve-per-cylinder timing layout (The M113.967 uses the famously durable SOHC 3-valve architecture with twin-spark ignition. Compared with the M272 family, it is mechanically simpler, less failure-prone, and far more tolerant of age and mileage. The timing system itself is not the major ownership fear here. The real issues are age-related leaks, ignition deterioration, cooling-system wear, and transmission neglect. In workshop terms, this is one of the last truly robust old-school Mercedes V8s before complexity and fragility increased significantly.)",
              injectionType: "Multi-Point Manifold Injection / Bosch-Mercedes Electronic Management (A mature, durable, and well-understood sequential port injection system. It is dramatically simpler than later direct-injection systems and does not suffer from high-pressure pump or piezo injector problems. The main fueling-related issues are aging MAF sensors, oxygen sensors, vacuum leaks, and ignition-related misfire behavior. For long-term ownership, this conventional injection system is a major advantage.)",
              engineLifespan: "300 000 - 450 000+ km (The M113 is one of Mercedes-Benz's best-regarded naturally aspirated V8 engines. The core engine is famously durable, and most ownership pain comes from surrounding systems rather than internal weakness. With regular oil service, a healthy cooling system, and prompt leak repair, the M113 can comfortably outlast the body and suspension restoration cycle. Cheap cars are usually dangerous because of neglected supporting systems, not because the bottom end is weak.)",
