    yearRange: "2005-2010",
    timingType: "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder chain-driven diesel timing layout (The OM642.920 is a modern Mercedes V6 diesel from the era when the brand was moving from older inline diesel refinement into a more compact and more sophisticated V6 format. Its DOHC 24-valve layout gives it strong breathing for a diesel, smooth power delivery, and good emissions control potential for its time. The chain-driven timing system is fundamentally durable, and unlike some gasoline engines of the same era, the timing hardware is not usually the first thing buyers worry about. In real ownership, this engine is defined much more by the health of its oil seals, intake hardware, emissions systems, and turbo support components than by core chain anxiety. The long block itself has a solid reputation when serviced correctly. What matters most is whether the surrounding systems have aged gracefully or been neglected. That is why a documented OM642 car is worth much more than a cosmetically shiny mystery example.)",
    injectionType: "Common-Rail Direct Injection with piezo injectors (This note matters in English because the engine’s refinement, emissions quality, and cold-start behavior depend heavily on the condition of the piezo injectors and the overall fuel system. The piezo setup allows fast and precise injection events, which helps the engine feel smoother and more modern than older diesel units. The downside is that injector-related faults are not especially cheap when they appear. Good fuel quality, clean system health, and proper diagnostic work matter here. When the injectors are healthy, the OM642 feels impressively quiet, strong, and flexible for a mid-2000s luxury diesel. When they are not, the engine can become uneven, smoky, or expensive in a very diesel-specific way.)",
    engineLifespan: "300 000 - 450 000 km (The OM642 can cover very serious mileage when maintained properly, which is one of the reasons it became such an important Mercedes diesel engine. The core engine is usually not what ends the life of the car. Much more often, oil cooler seal leaks, intake issues, EGR contamination, DPF-related problems, turbo hardware wear, and transmission neglect determine whether ownership remains pleasant. A well-kept OM642 can remain smooth and strong deep into high mileage. A neglected one can still drive well enough to fool buyers while hiding a long and expensive list of deferred diesel repairs. That makes service history absolutely central to buying decisions. The best examples feel like durable long-distance tools. The worst examples feel like luxury diesels waiting to invoice you.)",
    turboType: "Single turbocharger with variable turbine geometry (VTG) (This note matters in English because the VTG turbo is a major reason the engine feels strong from low rpm without needing a large-displacement V8-style powerband. Variable geometry helps give the OM642 its broad and useful torque delivery, especially in a heavy premium chassis like the CLS. It improves drivability and makes the car feel effortless in normal traffic and on the highway. However, the turbo system still depends on clean oil, healthy actuator behavior, and sensible thermal treatment. Soot buildup, actuator issues, or oil-related neglect can reduce response and raise repair costs. When healthy, the turbo makes the engine feel far more muscular than its horsepower figure suggests.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch / Mercedes diesel engine management with common-rail control, turbo management, and emissions integration (The ECU on the OM642 has to coordinate fuel pressure, injection timing, boost control, airflow calculation, glow support, and emissions behavior in a fairly advanced way for its generation. That gives the engine a refined and strong feel when everything is right, but it also means sensor drift and emissions-related faults can affect drivability more subtly than on older diesels. The system is not inherently troublesome, but it rewards correct diagnosis rather than guesswork. A bad MAF reading, sticky EGR behavior, DPF loading, or injector imbalance can all change the character of the car. In a healthy state, the engine feels clean, quiet, and expensive. In an unhealthy state, it feels lazy, smoky, or oddly inconsistent under load.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 4 diesel-era emissions configuration",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "255 - 270 hp (The OM642 responds well to a conservative diesel tune because the stock engine already has a broad torque foundation and the turbocharged diesel layout offers realistic software headroom. A mild remap can improve response and mid-range pulling power noticeably, but the value of tuning depends heavily on the health of the injectors, turbo, intake system, DPF, and transmission. This is not the kind of engine that should be tuned before deferred maintenance is solved. The hardware can support a sensible gain, but diesel tuning only feels good when the base car is already healthy. On a sorted car, the result can feel genuinely effortless. On a tired one, tuning simply amplifies underlying problems.)",
