        author: "Mercedes-Benz CLS w219 (https://skfb.ly/oWIRy) by Black Snow is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).",
        popupAnimationName: "",
        drivingExperience: "The fleet-tuned CLS 320 CDI should still feel like a proper Mercedes diesel grand tourer rather than a stripped-out economy special. The main difference is likely a slightly calmer performance ceiling rather than a missing torque character. In normal use it should remain smooth, quiet, and effortless on the motorway. The OM642 still suits the CLS very well because it delivers relaxed long-distance performance without needing revs. The appeal is mature torque and refinement, not drama. A good one feels expensive in the right ways.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From a mechanic’s perspective, the OM642.920 fleet version is mechanically the same core engine as the regular CLS 320 CDI. The note about a tax-reduced fleet version matters because, in English, it means the car was calibrated to satisfy taxation rules rather than to maximize showroom performance. That usually points to software-led output management rather than major hard-part changes. In daily use, the engine should still feel torquey and relaxed. The lower power figure does not suddenly make the OM642 simple or cheap to neglect. Oil cooler seals remain a known weak point. Swirl flap wear and intake contamination still matter. EGR and DPF problems can still stack up on short-trip fleet cars. Piezo injectors still need clean fuel and proper diagnosis. The VTG turbo still depends on good oil and healthy boost plumbing. Fleet history can be helpful if servicing was strict and mileage was mostly motorway. Fleet history can be terrible if the car had long intervals and no preventive work. The 7G-Tronic remains a major part of ownership quality. Buy documentation, not just a lower-tax badge or a lower-output story. If maintained correctly, this version can still be an excellent high-mileage long-distance CLS.",
        repairCostEstimate: "1 500 - 3 000 EUR / year (The engine itself is durable, but OM642 support-system issues and deferred maintenance still define the cost profile.)"
