        yearRange: "2009-2010",
        timingType: "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder chain-driven diesel timing layout (The CLS 350 CDI keeps the same OM642.920 mechanical base as the late CLS 320 CDI, which is exactly what the rebadged note is trying to communicate. This is still the same refined Mercedes V6 diesel architecture, with the same strong long-distance character and the same familiar support-system risks. The long block remains fundamentally durable when serviced correctly. Oil cooler seals, intake hardware, emissions condition, turbo health, and transmission maintenance still matter far more than the badge on the trunk. That makes the CLS 350 CDI reassuring from a technical continuity perspective. It is not a mystery variant. It is a late-nameplate OM642 CLS.)",
        injectionType: "Common-Rail Direct Injection with piezo injectors (In English, the rebadged note means Mercedes changed the nameplate strategy, not the fundamental injection-system character. The engine still relies on precise piezo injectors for smooth combustion, low-noise operation, and the refined diesel feel that makes the car convincing in luxury use. The same injector strengths and cost risks remain. Good fuel quality and proper diagnostics are still important. A healthy example feels polished and muscular. A weak injector set makes the rebadge story irrelevant very quickly because the car simply feels like a tired OM642.)",
        engineLifespan: "300 000 - 450 000 km (The late-nameplate CLS 350 CDI can cover very high mileage because it is still the familiar OM642 in a grand-touring Mercedes application. The engine is not usually killed by core bottom-end weakness. Instead, ownership quality is shaped by oil cooler leaks, swirl-flap and intake issues, EGR contamination, DPF loading, boost leaks, injector quality, and transmission service history. That means the same buying rules still apply as on the CLS 320 CDI. History matters more than badges. A sorted one can be a superb long-distance car. A neglected one is still an OM642 repair project in expensive clothing.)",
        turboType: "Single turbocharger with variable turbine geometry (VTG) (The rebadge does not change the VTG turbo concept that gives the OM642 its broad and satisfying torque delivery. The engine still feels strong from low rpm and very well matched to the relaxed, high-speed CLS character. In English, the note simply means the car was renamed under Mercedes’ later naming strategy rather than being re-engineered around a different turbo system. Boost behavior still depends on clean oil, healthy actuator control, sound boost hoses, and sensible heat management. A healthy turbo makes the car feel much stronger than the modest horsepower figure suggests. A weak turbo makes it feel like a diesel in decline.)",
        ecuType: "Bosch / Mercedes diesel engine management with later model-name strategy but familiar OM642 control logic (The ECU environment remains recognizably OM642. Fuel pressure, injection timing, boost control, airflow, glow strategy, and emissions behavior are still handled in the same general Mercedes diesel framework. In English, the note means this was basically a renamed late CLS 320 CDI rather than an all-new mechanical package. That is useful because diagnosis, ownership logic, and known faults are already well understood. The badge says 350 CDI, but the technician still sees a known OM642 story. That makes the car easier to evaluate than a one-year oddball would be. Known engines are safer engines when you buy used.)",
        euroStandard: "Euro 4 diesel-era emissions configuration, late C219 rebadged naming phase",
        maxPowerStockInternals: "255 - 270 hp (The CLS 350 CDI has the same practical tuning logic as the late CLS 320 CDI because the engine base is the same OM642. A conservative tune can sharpen the mid-range, but only when the car is already healthy. Injector condition, DPF state, turbo control, intake cleanliness, and transmission quality matter more than the rear badge. The engine has real diesel headroom, but tuning is not a fix for deferred maintenance. A strong car responds well. A weak car becomes expensive faster.)",
