        customLightNames: { "headlights": ["headlights"], "taillights": ["taillights1", "taillights2"], "drllight": ["drls"] },
        author: "Mercedes-Benz CLS w219 (https://skfb.ly/oWIRy) by Black Snow is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).",
        popupAnimationName: "",
        drivingExperience: "The CLS 350 CDI feels like a late-nameplate continuation of everything good about the diesel C219 formula. It is smooth, relaxed, and very competent on long motorway drives. The OM642 gives it effortless mid-range thrust without needing aggressive revs. In daily use, it feels refined rather than sporty. The appeal is quiet torque, premium cruising, and long-distance ease. A good one feels like an understated grand tourer.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From a mechanic’s perspective, the CLS 350 CDI badge change is mostly a naming story, not a different engine story. The note matters because, in English, it means Mercedes simply rebadged the earlier CLS 320 CDI under a newer naming strategy. Under the skin, you are still dealing with the familiar OM642.920. That is good news because the strengths and weaknesses are already well understood. The engine still delivers strong mid-range diesel torque and refined cruising manners. It still carries the same OM642 risk profile around oil cooler seals. Swirl flap and intake contamination issues do not disappear because of the new badge. EGR and DPF health still matter, especially on urban cars. Piezo injector quality still shapes refinement and repair cost. The VTG turbo still needs clean oil and leak-free charge plumbing. The 7G-Tronic is still the correct partner, but only when serviced properly. Buyers should not pay a big mechanical premium just because the rear badge says 350 CDI. They should pay for condition, history, and evidence of preventive maintenance. A good rebadged car is just as satisfying as a good late CLS 320 CDI. A bad one is the same expensive OM642 project with a different trunk lid script.",
        repairCostEstimate: "1 500 - 3 000 EUR / year (The badge changed, but the ownership math stayed essentially OM642.)"
