    yearRange: "2009-2010",
    timingType: "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder chain-driven diesel timing layout (The OM642.920 fleet-spec CLS 350 CDI uses the same core Mercedes V6 diesel architecture as the better-known retail versions, so this should be understood as a calibrated market or taxation variant rather than a fundamentally different engine family. Its DOHC 24-valve chain-driven layout gives it a refined and modern diesel character for the period, with strong breathing, good low-end torque delivery, and long-distance cruising ability. The timing layout itself is not usually the main ownership concern on this engine. In real life, the condition of the oil cooler seals, intake hardware, emissions-related systems, turbocharger support components, and transmission service history matters far more than the basic cam drive design. The long block remains fundamentally strong when maintained properly. A healthy example still feels smooth, muscular, and very Mercedes-like. A neglected one still behaves like an OM642 waiting to present a stacked diesel repair bill.)",
    injectionType: "Common-Rail Direct Injection with piezo injectors (This matters because even in the lower-output fleet version, the engine still depends heavily on injection precision for refinement, cold-start quality, emissions behavior, and smooth torque delivery. The piezo injector setup is one of the reasons the OM642 feels more modern and polished than older Mercedes diesels. The downside is that injector-related issues are never especially cheap once properly diagnosed. Good fuel quality, proper filtration, and correct diagnostic work remain essential. When injector health is good, the car feels expensive and effortless. When it is not, the engine quickly becomes rougher, smokier, and more costly than the power figure alone would suggest.)",
    engineLifespan: "300 000 - 450 000 km (The fleet-tuned OM642 can still deliver very high mileage because the underlying engine remains one of Mercedes-Benz’s most important modern V6 diesels. The core structure is durable, and most unpleasant ownership stories begin in the surrounding systems rather than in the crankcase itself. Oil cooler seal leaks, intake and swirl-flap faults, EGR contamination, DPF loading, turbo-control issues, and gearbox neglect usually define whether the car remains satisfying or becomes expensive. Fleet history can be either a strength or a weakness depending on how the car was maintained. A motorway-driven, properly serviced fleet example may be stronger than a low-mileage short-trip private one. A neglected fleet car can hide a great deal of deferred cost under an otherwise calm driving impression.)",
    turboType: "Single turbocharger with variable turbine geometry (VTG) and intercooler (The VTG setup is a major reason why the OM642 feels torque-rich and flexible in a heavy premium chassis like the CLS. Even in a lower-output calibration, the engine should still deliver strong low- and mid-range response without needing excessive revs. The fleet-tuned nature of the engine does not remove the usefulness of the turbo system; it mainly reshapes how the available power is delivered. Good oil quality, healthy actuator behavior, clean boost plumbing, and sensible thermal treatment remain critical. Soot buildup, actuator wear, or charge-air leaks can quickly make the engine feel flat and disappointing. When healthy, the turbo helps the car feel far stronger than a simple 211 hp number might suggest.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch / Mercedes diesel engine management with market-specific fleet calibration (The ECU on this version still has to coordinate fuel pressure, injection timing, boost control, airflow management, glow support, and emissions strategy in a fairly advanced way for its generation. What changes here is not the basic control philosophy, but the output strategy and likely torque shaping intended for taxation or fleet positioning. That means the engine may feel slightly calmer or softer than a full retail-output version, but not mechanically alien. Diagnosis still needs to be proper and data-based. MAF issues, EGR behavior, DPF saturation, and injector imbalance can all alter the car’s character significantly. In healthy condition, it remains smooth and refined. In unhealthy condition, it becomes another expensive modern diesel with subtle but meaningful drivability problems.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 4 diesel-era emissions configuration, post-renaming market-specific fleet calibration",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "245 - 260 hp (The 211 hp fleet version still sits on the same OM642 hardware family, so it should respond well to a conservative tune if the car is healthy. In theory, part of the missing output is likely calibration rather than hard mechanical limitation. In practice, tuning only makes sense after confirming injector balance, turbo condition, DPF health, intake cleanliness, and transmission service quality. This is not an engine that rewards shortcut tuning on a tired base car. The hardware can usually support a sensible gain. But the real win is always restoring a healthy diesel before attempting to make it quicker.)",
