    yearRange: "2009-2010",
    timingType: "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder chain-driven diesel timing layout (The late Grand Edition version still rests on the same OM642.920 diesel foundation, but in this form it is presented as a stronger and more exclusive end-of-run variant. The DOHC 24-valve chain-driven V6 architecture remains one of the most refined Mercedes diesel layouts of its era, offering a strong blend of torque, smoothness, and high-speed composure. The core engine design is not what usually frightens informed buyers. What matters is the condition of the oil cooler seals, intake hardware, emissions system, turbocharger support components, and gearbox. In a healthy car, this engine feels muscular, expensive, and genuinely fast for a diesel luxury coupe. In a neglected one, the same familiar OM642 ownership traps still apply. The late special-edition positioning improves desirability, but it does not erase the mechanical rules.)",
    injectionType: "Common-Rail Direct Injection with piezo injectors (In this stronger Grand Edition form, the piezo injector system becomes even more central to the engine’s premium feel. Injection precision helps give the car its smooth combustion, quiet running, and effortless high-torque character, especially at motorway speeds and under strong mid-range acceleration. The system is one of the reasons the OM642 never feels like an old-fashioned diesel when it is healthy. The downside is still cost. Injector faults are rarely cheap once confirmed, and poor fuel-system care only makes the situation worse. When everything is right, this engine feels clean, modern, and forceful. When injector quality falls away, the entire luxury-diesel illusion collapses quickly.)",
    engineLifespan: "300 000 - 450 000 km (Even in the stronger Grand Edition guise, the OM642 remains a high-mileage-capable engine if serviced properly. The extra output does not change the basic truth that the long block is generally durable while the surrounding systems determine whether ownership remains enjoyable. Oil cooler seals, intake contamination, EGR behavior, DPF health, injector condition, turbo response, and transmission service history still define the difference between an excellent car and an expensive one. A very good Grand Edition can feel like one of the most complete diesel C219 variants ever made. A poor one can still carry all the usual OM642 liabilities with the added cost expectations of a more desirable trim. History matters at least as much as rarity.)",
    turboType: "Single turbocharger with variable turbine geometry (VTG), intercooler, and stronger output calibration (The VTG turbo system is one of the biggest reasons this Grand Edition version feels meaningfully stronger than a standard lower-output OM642 application. Variable geometry helps the engine build torque early, sustain it usefully across the mid-range, and deliver the kind of effortless performance that suits the CLS chassis perfectly. In this form the car should feel fast, broad-shouldered, and very relaxed under load. But the same rules still apply. The turbo system depends on clean oil, healthy actuator behavior, good boost plumbing, and careful heat management. Any weakness in those areas quickly reduces the car from special to merely expensive. A healthy VTG setup is what gives this version its effortless authority.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch / Mercedes diesel engine management with factory higher-output calibration (The stronger Grand Edition tune means the ECU is working with a more assertive version of the OM642 strategy while still managing common-rail pressure, injection timing, boost, airflow, glow support, and emissions behavior in the same basic architecture. In English, this matters because the engine should not be treated as a separate family; it is still an OM642 with a more ambitious factory calibration. That makes it desirable, but it also means diagnosis still has to be sharp. Sensor drift, injector irregularity, DPF load, airflow faults, and boost-control issues can all blunt the extra performance. When everything is healthy, the result feels genuinely quick and polished. When it is not, the car loses the very quality that makes the Grand Edition attractive.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 4 diesel-era emissions configuration",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "285 - 300 hp (Because this version already appears in stronger factory-output form, tuning headroom remains present but should be approached with more respect. The OM642 hardware still has realistic scope for additional power, but the quality of the base car matters even more at this level. Injector balance, DPF health, turbo condition, intake cleanliness, charge-air integrity, and gearbox service history all become decisive. A healthy Grand Edition can take a mild tune very well. A tired one should not be pushed harder until its known weak points are corrected. More power only feels premium when the engine is already operating cleanly and consistently.)",
