    valves: 24,
    compression: "12.0:1",
    yearRange: "2011-2014",
    timingType: "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder chain-driven gasoline timing layout (The M276.952 belongs to Mercedes-Benz’s modern naturally aspirated V6 era, where the brand aimed to combine traditional six-cylinder smoothness with direct injection, better efficiency, and cleaner emissions behavior than the older M272 family. Its DOHC 24-valve chain-driven layout gives it a refined and technically mature structure, and the basic mechanical concept is much more reassuring than many buyers expect when they hear the words direct injection and early BlueEFFICIENCY. The timing system is generally not the defining weakness of the engine in normal ownership, and the long block itself is fundamentally solid when oil changes are done properly and overheating has been avoided. What matters far more in real life is the condition of the cooling system, intake cleanliness, ignition hardware, sensor accuracy, and the broader service history. This is not a crude old-school Mercedes V6. It is a more sophisticated naturally aspirated engine that rewards proper maintenance and punishes neglect in subtler, more modern ways. A documented example is usually far more valuable than one that merely looks clean on the surface.)",
    injectionType: "Direct Injection gasoline system with CGI/stratified-charge-era combustion strategy (This matters because the note is really telling you that this is not just a simple port-injected V6 with ordinary fuel delivery. The direct-injection setup helps the engine feel efficient, crisp, and modern for its size, and it is one of the reasons the M276 can deliver strong real-world performance without forced induction. The downside is that fuel quality, injector behavior, intake cleanliness, and sensor precision all matter more than on older injection systems. Carbon buildup can become a believable long-term ownership consideration because fuel is no longer constantly washing the intake valves in the traditional port-injected way. When the system is healthy, the engine feels smooth, eager, and expensive. When it is not, rough running, mixture-related faults, and disappointing throttle response can make the whole car feel older than it should.)",
    engineLifespan: "250 000 - 400 000 km (The M276.952 is fully capable of serious mileage if it receives the kind of servicing that modern Mercedes gasoline engines require. The long block itself is not usually the first thing that ends the useful life of the car. Much more often, coolant leaks, thermostat wear, ignition faults, carbon accumulation, oil seepage, intake-related issues, and neglected transmission maintenance shape the ownership experience. A well-kept example can remain very smooth and refined deep into high mileage. A neglected one can still sound healthy enough during a short test drive while hiding an expensive backlog of smaller but cumulative problems. This is why service history matters so much on the pre-facelift C218. The best cars feel like durable, high-speed grand tourers. The worst ones feel like premium sedans that slowly tax their owners through a series of individually manageable but collectively annoying repairs.)",
    turboType: "Naturally aspirated with variable intake and modern airflow management (The lack of turbocharging is one of the defining character traits of this version of the CLS 350. In a world that quickly moved toward smaller turbocharged engines, the M276.952 gives you a more linear, more natural power delivery that rises cleanly with revs instead of leaning on boost. That makes the engine feel smoother and more predictable, especially in everyday driving and part-throttle use. It also removes an entire layer of turbocharger heat, plumbing, and boost-control complexity from the ownership equation. The trade-off is obvious: it does not deliver the same effortless low-rpm shove as a strong turbo engine. But what it gives back is cleaner response, more progressive character, and a very polished naturally aspirated V6 feel.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch / Mercedes gasoline engine management with direct injection, variable valve timing, and efficiency-focused combustion control (The ECU on the M276 has to coordinate direct-injection pressure and timing, ignition control, airflow, variable cam adjustment, throttle behavior, knock adaptation, and emissions-related monitoring in a fairly advanced way for its time. That gives the engine a refined and polished personality when everything works correctly, but it also means sensor drift and mixture-related issues can affect drivability more subtly than on older Mercedes gasoline engines. The system is not inherently bad, but it rewards correct diagnosis rather than random parts replacement. A weak ignition component, dirty intake path, incorrect airflow reading, or cooling-related operating-temperature issue can all change how premium the engine feels. In a healthy state, the car feels smooth, linear, and expensive. In an unhealthy state, it can feel hesitant, lumpy at idle, or strangely flat for a 306 hp V6.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 5-era naturally aspirated direct-injection gasoline configuration",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "330 - 345 hp (Because this is a naturally aspirated direct-injection V6, tuning expectations need to remain realistic. The engine does not offer the easy headline gains of a turbocharged platform, and software alone will not transform it into something dramatically faster. A mild calibration, combined with perfect ignition health, clean intake paths, and ideally a freer-flowing but sensible intake/exhaust setup, can sharpen response and add a modest amount of power. But the real appeal of this engine is not extreme tuning headroom. It is refined stock character, smooth delivery, and a properly engineered naturally aspirated response. The best tuned examples feel cleaner and slightly more urgent. The worst tuned examples simply become noisier without gaining much of real value.)",
