      "vulnerabilities": [
        "The M254.920 is vulnerable to the usual modern high-output 2.0-liter petrol ownership pattern, where several smaller condition-related issues can overlap before one obvious failure appears. A car can still feel quick while hiding ignition weakness, early intake contamination, minor charge-air leakage, and mild cooling-system drift at the same time.",
        "This engine is heavily dependent on maintenance quality because turbocharged direct-injection petrol units are less tolerant of stretched oil intervals and neglected ancillary systems than older, simpler Mercedes petrol engines. Long oil intervals, ignored coolant ageing, and delayed ignition service usually make later repairs more expensive than they needed to be.",
        "Because the engine, emissions hardware, and integrated hybrid support system are tightly linked, the car is especially vulnerable to poor diagnosis. Owners who replace random parts reactively usually spend more money than owners who diagnose the condition of the whole drivetrain properly from the beginning.",
        "Thermal management remains a secondary vulnerability because the M254.920 is a compact turbo engine producing strong output in a premium executive car. When temperature control, oil quality, and charge-air efficiency are all healthy, the drivetrain feels very polished, but when they are not, the engine feels harsher and more stressed than the brochure figures suggest.",
        "The ISG-based support layer adds a newer type of ownership risk compared with older Mercedes petrol engines. It does not make the car unreliable by default, but it does mean that a clean, smooth test drive is not the same thing as having a cheap long-term example."
      ],
