    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven inline-six timing layout. The OM656.929 gives the C257 CLS 350 d the kind of drivetrain character that suits the platform far better than the four-cylinder diesels, because the 3.0-liter inline-six combines strong low-end torque with the smoother, more expensive-feeling delivery that a premium Mercedes fastback genuinely benefits from. In standard 286 hp form, the engine feels muscular, refined, and long-legged rather than dramatic. This is still a highly integrated modern Euro 6 diesel with bi-turbocharging, AdBlue, DPF, EGR, and substantial thermal-management complexity, so it is not a simple old-school six-cylinder diesel despite the strong core engine concept. Oil quality, charge-air integrity, emissions-system health, cooling stability, injector behavior, and transmission servicing all matter. A healthy OM656.929 feels effortless and premium. A neglected one becomes expensive through several interlinked diesel-system faults rather than one obvious failure.",
    "injectionType": "Common-rail direct-injection diesel system with high-pressure fuel delivery, intercooling, and bi-turbocharging. The OM656.929 relies on precise injector behavior and stable combustion quality to deliver its broad torque curve without losing refinement. Because it is a modern emissions-heavy straight-six diesel, fuel quality, injector health, EGR cleanliness, DPF condition, and SCR / AdBlue function matter just as much as the mechanical engine itself. When healthy, the drivetrain feels rich, smooth, and effortless in normal driving. When fuel, air, boost, or emissions quality drifts, the car quickly loses the polished character that defines a proper CLS 350 d.",
    "engineLifespan": "280,000 to 450,000 km. The OM656.929 is capable of very strong long-term mileage when maintained correctly, because the inline-six layout is inherently smooth and well-suited to executive-car duty. Oil-change discipline, correct low-SAPS oil, cooling-system stability, charge-air integrity, emissions-system health, and gearbox servicing all influence ownership quality. Cars used mainly for long-distance driving usually age much better than short-trip urban examples with repeated cold starts and interrupted regenerations. A properly maintained example can remain an excellent long-distance drivetrain. A neglected one often becomes expensive because several medium-to-large diesel and support-system repairs begin to overlap.",
    "turboType": "Bi-turbocharged inline-six diesel engine with intercooling. The turbo system is a major part of what makes the OM656.929 feel so complete in the CLS, because it gives the engine a broad and effortless torque delivery across the real-world rpm range. The result is a drivetrain that feels strong and expensive rather than highly strung. Boost integrity, charge-air sealing, oil quality, and thermal stability remain important with age. This is a very convincing modern Mercedes six-cylinder diesel when healthy. It still depends on disciplined maintenance to stay that way.",
    "ecuType": "Modern Mercedes/Bosch-style diesel engine management with common-rail injection control, turbocharger coordination, EGR management, DPF regeneration logic, SCR / AdBlue dosing, and integrated thermal-management strategy. The ECU has to coordinate combustion quality, emissions efficiency, turbo behavior, and transmission interaction as one system, which is why subtle sensor drift or injector, boost, or emissions faults can affect drivability long before the engine feels obviously unhealthy. Accurate diagnosis matters much more than guesswork. In healthy condition, the drivetrain feels extremely polished and effortless. In tired condition, it can feel heavier, rougher, and more interrupted than a premium inline-six Mercedes should.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6d diesel configuration with DPF and SCR / AdBlue support",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "340-370 hp. The OM656.929 has useful tuning headroom because the 2.9-liter inline-six already has strong airflow and torque capacity in standard form, but it is also a modern emissions-heavy diesel that rewards moderation. A conservative tune can make the car feel even more effortless in the mid-range, but it only makes sense on examples with stable cooling, healthy DPF and SCR systems, clean intake flow, strong injector behavior, and no charge-air weakness. Aggressive tuning mainly adds thermal and emissions stress. This engine rewards condition-first tuning rather than headline chasing.",
