        "yearRange": "2018-2021",
        "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven inline-six timing layout. This fleet-focused OM656.929 version keeps the same 3.0-liter straight-six diesel base as the standard CLS 350 d, but uses a softer software calibration for cost-sensitive and administrative fleet environments in EU markets. The result is a calmer and slightly less urgent drivetrain while still retaining the deep low-end torque and executive-car refinement that make the inline-six such a good match for the C257 platform. This remains a highly integrated modern Euro 6 diesel with bi-turbocharging, AdBlue, DPF, EGR, and meaningful thermal-management complexity, so it is not a low-stress old-school diesel despite the smoother output delivery. Oil quality, charge-air integrity, emissions-system health, cooling stability, injector behavior, and transmission servicing all still matter greatly. A healthy example feels smooth, rich, and understated. A neglected one becomes expensive through several interlinked diesel-system faults rather than one obvious failure.",
        "injectionType": "Common-rail direct-injection diesel system with high-pressure fuel delivery, intercooling, and bi-turbocharging managed through a softer fleet-oriented output strategy. The OM656.929 in 272 hp fleet form still depends on precise injector behavior and stable combustion quality to preserve its smooth and torquey executive-car character. Because it is still a modern emissions-heavy straight-six diesel, fuel quality, injector health, EGR cleanliness, DPF condition, and SCR / AdBlue function matter just as much as on the stronger versions. The reduced output does not remove the need for proper maintenance. When healthy, the drivetrain feels smooth and substantial. When fuel, air, boost, or emissions quality drifts, the car loses polish surprisingly quickly.",
        "engineLifespan": "290,000 to 460,000 km. The softer-calibrated OM656.929 can still achieve very good long-term mileage when maintained correctly, because the lower-output tune does not fundamentally change the strong core quality of the inline-six diesel platform. Oil-change discipline, correct low-SAPS oil, cooling-system stability, charge-air integrity, emissions-system health, and gearbox servicing all influence ownership quality. Long-distance use typically suits this engine far better than repeated short cold cycles. The softer software tune gives a little more comfort margin, but not enough to excuse neglected maintenance. A properly maintained example can remain an outstanding long-distance drivetrain. A neglected one often becomes expensive through stacked turbo, intake, emissions, and support-system repair work.",
        "turboType": "Bi-turbocharged inline-six diesel engine with intercooling. The turbo system is still a major part of what makes the fleet-calibrated OM656.929 feel expensive and usefully strong even though the output is intentionally reduced. The result is a drivetrain that feels smooth and broad rather than especially fast. Boost integrity, charge-air sealing, oil quality, and thermal stability remain important with age. This is still a convincing modern Mercedes six-cylinder diesel when healthy. It still depends on disciplined maintenance to stay that way.",
        "ecuType": "Modern Mercedes/Bosch-style diesel engine management with common-rail injection control, turbocharger coordination, EGR management, DPF regeneration logic, SCR / AdBlue dosing, and fleet-oriented lower-output calibration strategy. The ECU still has to coordinate combustion quality, emissions efficiency, turbo behavior, and transmission interaction as one system, which is why subtle sensor drift or injector, boost, or emissions faults can affect drivability well before the engine feels obviously unhealthy. Accurate diagnosis matters much more than guesswork. In healthy condition, the drivetrain feels smooth and understated. In tired condition, it can feel flatter, rougher, and more electronically interrupted than a proper fleet-calibrated CLS 350 d should.",
        "euroStandard": "Euro 6d diesel configuration with DPF, SCR / AdBlue support, and fleet-oriented lower-output calibration",
        "maxPowerStockInternals": "335-360 hp. The OM656.929 still has useful tuning headroom in this fleet-detuned form because much of the reduced output comes from software strategy rather than a fundamentally weaker engine concept, but it is also a modern emissions-heavy diesel that rewards moderation. A conservative tune can recover much of the missing real-world response, but it only makes sense on examples with stable cooling, healthy DPF and SCR systems, clean intake flow, strong injector behavior, and no charge-air weakness. Aggressive tuning mainly increases thermal and emissions stress. This engine rewards condition-first tuning rather than headline chasing.",
