            "customLightNames": {"headlights": ["headlights"],"taillights": ["taillights.003"],"drllight": ["drls.008"]},
            "author": ".",
            "popupAnimationName": "",
            "drivingExperience": "The late Euro 6d OM656.929 gives the CLS 400 d the kind of drivetrain character that still genuinely suits the C257 platform. The straight-six smoothness and massive torque delivery make the car feel relaxed, expensive, and effortlessly quick in a way that the smaller diesels cannot fully match. In everyday driving, the engine feels dense and fluid rather than dramatic, but under load it delivers very strong real-world pace without any sense of strain. It is slightly more emissions-calibrated in feel than the early version, but still clearly a premium six-cylinder Mercedes diesel. The whole car still feels like it was built around this engine.",
            "mechanicVerdict": "From a technical perspective, the late Euro 6d OM656.929 remains one of the most convincing modern Mercedes diesel engines for executive-car use because it combines the inherent smoothness of an inline-six with the efficiency and flexibility of a current turbocharged Euro 6d diesel package. In the CLS 400 d, it gives the C257 the sort of effortless torque delivery, low-noise cruising behavior, and natural premium refinement that make the platform feel fully resolved rather than merely competent. The important reality, however, is that this is still a complex modern drivetrain, and the ownership experience is shaped much more by maintenance quality than by the elegance of the engine concept alone. Oil service discipline, cooling-system stability, injector quality, charge-air integrity, intake cleanliness, gearbox service history, and the health of the DPF and SCR support systems all matter if the car is expected to remain both smooth and economically sensible over time. The engine is so refined when healthy that it can hide the early stages of deterioration better than a rougher four-cylinder would, which means buyers often underestimate how important good diagnosis is on these cars. A strong test drive is not enough on its own, because an OM656 can still feel excellent while early boost leaks, mild injector imbalance, thermal-management drift, or gearbox service neglect are already beginning to build future cost. When maintained properly, the late Euro 6d OM656.929 is an outstanding modern Mercedes engine and one of the best drivetrain matches for the C257. When maintained carelessly, it follows the familiar modern premium-car pattern where several medium-to-large repair items overlap into one expensive correction phase.",
            "repairCostEstimate": "3,200-5,900 EUR per year. This assumes a generally healthy OM656.929 late Euro 6d CLS 400 d example with realistic maintenance needs, including oil-service discipline, fuel-system upkeep, cooling-system attention, emissions-system monitoring, transmission servicing, and occasional deeper diagnostic work."
